My own recollection is a bit vague, but the gist of it is that VMS V1
shipped with native RMS-32 support for *sequential* files (and possibly
relative files, but then who cared?) but not indexed files, which were
supported by an escape that allowed native VMS code to run RMS-11 in
compatibility mode.

As for 16-bit-ness, when RMS-11 was designed initial work on VAX had already
begun, and RMS-11 (as well as RMS-32, when it arrived) was designed to
accommodate *large* (well, for that time) file systems, using 4-byte values
for in-file block offsets, for example (that's where the 6-byte RFA field
size comes from, and it was that size in RMS-11 V1.0).  So I'm not sure
where the idea that *either* RMS implementation was a '16-bit file system'
came from.

- bill

