"Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:

> As for 16-bit-ness, when RMS-11 was designed initial work on VAX had already
> begun, and RMS-11 (as well as RMS-32, when it arrived) was designed to
> accommodate *large* (well, for that time) file systems, using 4-byte values
> for in-file block offsets, for example (that's where the 6-byte RFA field
> size comes from, and it was that size in RMS-11 V1.0).  So I'm not sure
> where the idea that *either* RMS implementation was a '16-bit file system'
> came from.

Yes, I'd say that all object sizes _except_record_lengths_ have stood the 
test of time very well, and the upgrade to lift the 8 GB volume limit seemed
quite simple. OTOH, I'd also say that RMS's 16 bit record lengths, while 
possibly saving a byte or two here and there, already were a limitation
with the earliest versions of VMS: see the introduction of segmented records
in the Fortran RTL, which requires unnecessary overhead both in time and in
space. At least the option to allow a version of files with variable-length
records where the record lengths are 32 bits long would have been an excellent
idea right from the start. Would that still be practical now - e.g., does the 
bit field in the file header containing the record type have unused values?

	Jan



