Welcome to ala carte configurations.

The part number you gave (DA-55NJA-EA) is a "system building block" (or SBB)
and is only the beginning of a complete system.  That particular part number
represents a DS20 6/500 with a single CPU, 512mb of memory, a VMS base
license, pre-installed operating system, and the "Enterprise Integration
Server" license and media (which includes TCP/IP and a bunch of other
stuff).  The list price for that SBB is $18,983.

The additional CPU module is part number KN310-AC and lists at $11,000.

You did want VMS, right?  There are different part numbers for the TRU64
flavors.

According to the Systems & Options Catalog (or SOC), there are a few
mandatory options that have to be selected.  You need to pick a system disk,
ethernet adapter, and 2 SCSI controllers in order to make a complete system.
Not knowing what it is you need or want, I'll just pick a couple of things
semi-randomnly.

For the system disk, let's say a 9.1gb, 10k rpm, UltraSCSI drive.  That
would be a DS-RZ1DD-VW.  List price is $817.

To make this short, I'll pick a KZPCM-DA to satisfy the rest of the
mandatory options.  This is a two-channel UltraSCSI adapter with a 10/100
Ethernet controller and its list price is $1,095.

So, that lists out at  $18,983 + 11,000 + 817 + 1,095  =  $31,895.

The DS20E SBB is part number DY-56NRA-EA for the same single CPU, 512mb box.
It requires selection of pedestal or rack-mount cabinet, disk drive cage,
system disk, one SCSI controller, Ethernet adapter, and power cord.  If
you'd like, pick the part numbers from the SOC and post them and I'll be
happy to lookup the list price for you.

If you hadn't found it already, the SOCs are online at
http://www.digital.com/info/SOHOME/SOHOMEHM.HTM and are in PDF and WORD
format.  They're kind of lengthy (17 pages for each of the DS20 and DS20E
specs).  They describe what's included with the base SBB part numbers as
well as the mandatory options and the variety of part numbers for those (as
well as the legal configuration rules).  So while it's tedious, the
information is all there to come up with a valid configuration.

Compaq also has a Windows-based configuration tool that you can download
from http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/configure.html which supposedly will
make sure you don't misconfigure the system.  I used it once a while ago and
remember that it didn't let me remove something from the configuration I was
building, so it's not quite a perfect tool.  It also provides -- if I
remember correctly -- the list pricing for the complete system.

Is this fun yet?

You'll have to order media, documentation, and user licenses seperately.
That can be a whole other adventure once you decide what you need, but it's
really just going through the motions of picking out part numbers.  It is
all online, somewhere.

Then, you get to pick an authorized reseller to buy it all from.  And, no,
I'm not one, so this isn't a plug for business.  I just needed the mental
break from my real work.  Whoever you select should be able to do all this
for you if you tell them what you need in the system.  If you want to save
some bucks, though, figure out the configuration yourself and go to one of
the authorized used/refurbished dealers (there are only two of them, so
picking shouldn't be difficult).  They're not so good at configuring, in my
experience, but give them part numbers and they'll usually come up with some
decent numbers.


Enjoy!
Frank




