:>   "bit-ness" found outside of marketing literature... :-)
:
:Well, it wasn't really a question - if you go back long enough you'll see 
:that I made a s(n)ide remark about RMS meaning a 16-bit file subsystem on 
:these newsgroups.

  Leading with the worst case discussion on "bit-ness".... Records in 
  various file organizations are largely limited to lengths circa 32K.
  (This is due to word-length fields encoded directly into disk record 
  structures with various file formats.)

  Various other RMS and XQP fields are longwords...

  The file itself system presently deals with volumes upwards of fifty 
  gigabytes or more.  The architectural (theoretical) limit on volume 
  size is around two terabytes, but to avoid the potential for any 
  signed-comparision "overflows" that might be lurking, we generally 
  discuss a one terabyte volume limit...  (I haven't seen anyone that
  has assembled a one terabyte RAID volume...)
  
  Folks here in OpenVMS Engineering have been discussing what we expect 
  high-volume and large-scale applications need for future file structures 
  and future disk structures as part of the file system work I referenced 
  earlier.  Some folks need/want traditional RMS access, and others simply 
  want XQP-level file system support and their own block-level access and
  use their own file structure...

:However, that's based on work done 12-14 years ago, and I certainly cannot 
:*recall* the exact assembler code I was wading through.

  The VMS File System Internals is a good resource for these sorts of
  questions...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
