try ..
>>> B DKA100
or
>>> B DKA200

when one is correct,  just do
>>> SET BOOT DKAx00
to save the correct default boot disk.

If you have no idea of the system password,
>>> B DKAx00 /1
SYSBOOT> SET UAF 1
SYSBOOT> CONTINUE
when boot has finished, press return to be prompted for username
Username : SYSTEM
Password : <just type a return>
Password : <type a second return>
You are connected with the system alternate user database.
$ define SYSUAF sys$system:SYSUAF.DAT
$ MC AUTHORIZE
UAF> modify SYSTEM /password=<your new password>
UAF> exit
You modified the main user database ...
$ mc SYSGEN
SYSGEN> USE CURRENT
SYSGEN> SET UAF 0
SYSGEN> WRITE CURRENT
SYSGEN> EXIT
You just restored the main user database as the default

Good luck ...

