When RMS-11 (and the initial cut at RMS-32) was designed in 1975, the idea
that any significant number of applications would really need individual
records larger than 32 KB (as opposed to, for example, using block I/O to
create their own large internal structures with better control over
fetch/store granularity) was, shall we say, not at the forefront of
concerns - though if as you suggest Fortran had to develop a work-around
'way back then it does look like an oversight.

Ditto for things like indexed file buckets larger than 64 (32?) KB, which,
given that records couldn't cross bucket boundaries, was consistent.  Think
about disk transfer rates back then, well before their Moore's-law-like rate
of improvement was well established:  neither decision had anything to do
with '16-bit-ness' - they were simple resource conservation measures at a
time when both memory and disk resources were far more precious than they
are 25 years later.

Introducing a new 32-bit-size variable-length record type in RMS over time,
however, would have been reasonable (given some real indication of need).
But, as I noted above, rolling your own using block I/O has always been an
option as well (at least for sequential-file-like accessing), and isn't
really very difficult.

- bill

Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> wrote in
message news:y4yaac23lj.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de...
> "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:
>
> > As for 16-bit-ness, when RMS-11 was designed initial work on VAX had
already
> > begun, and RMS-11 (as well as RMS-32, when it arrived) was designed to
> > accommodate *large* (well, for that time) file systems, using 4-byte
values
> > for in-file block offsets, for example (that's where the 6-byte RFA
field
> > size comes from, and it was that size in RMS-11 V1.0).  So I'm not sure
> > where the idea that *either* RMS implementation was a '16-bit file
system'
> > came from.
>
> Yes, I'd say that all object sizes _except_record_lengths_ have stood the
> test of time very well, and the upgrade to lift the 8 GB volume limit
seemed
> quite simple. OTOH, I'd also say that RMS's 16 bit record lengths, while
> possibly saving a byte or two here and there, already were a limitation
> with the earliest versions of VMS: see the introduction of segmented
records
> in the Fortran RTL, which requires unnecessary overhead both in time and
in
> space. At least the option to allow a version of files with
variable-length
> records where the record lengths are 32 bits long would have been an
excellent
> idea right from the start. Would that still be practical now - e.g., does
the
> bit field in the file header containing the record type have unused
values?
>
> Jan





