> In article <84em6a$178$1@pyrite.mv.net>, "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com>
writes:
>
> > As for 16-bit-ness, when RMS-11 was designed initial work on VAX had
already
> > begun, and RMS-11 (as well as RMS-32, when it arrived) was designed to
> > accommodate *large* (well, for that time) file systems, using 4-byte
values
> > for in-file block offsets, for example (that's where the 6-byte RFA
field
> > size comes from, and it was that size in RMS-11 V1.0).  So I'm not sure
> > where the idea that *either* RMS implementation was a '16-bit file
system'
> > came from.
>
> It might be the fact that certain ODS-n 32-bit values are stored on
> disk as 2 16-bit halves, swapped from the natural VAX accessing order.
> The data (typically a block number) is still 32-bits, but it takes more
> than one instruction to fetch it for calculations.

True enough.  RMS could have chosen to mask the underlying historical ACP
representation by swapping the words before presenting them, but that
representation was also used by the RMS precursor on RSX (FCS-11) and
perpetuating it seemed the lesser evil, given the potential for confusion.

Paul Sture suggested that the perception might have come from the fact that
the initial RMS utilities on VMS were all compatibility-mode versions
inherited from RSX - also a possible explanation.

- bill

