In article <84gamr$ic8$1@pyrite.mv.net>, "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:

> Introducing a new 32-bit-size variable-length record type in RMS over time,
> however, would have been reasonable (given some real indication of need).
> But, as I noted above, rolling your own using block I/O has always been an
> option as well (at least for sequential-file-like accessing), and isn't
> really very difficult.

RMS packs multiple records into the same block, but when individual
records are more than 32767 bytes, wasting up to 511 bytes per record
should not deter one from using QIO directly.

In these days of buffer objects, one would not want it done in the
least-common-denominator style anyway.

