In article <386A391F.9884A84A@earthlink.net>,
David J. Dachtera <djesys.nospam@earthlink.net> wrote:
> Carlie Coats wrote:
> > ...
> > What I want is a good VMS/UNIX/Linux word-processing/desk-top
> > publishing package that permits multiple independent windows
> > into multiple documents, with proper referential integrity
> > (multiple windows into a document are windows into the *same*
> > document), and that can import at least reasonably well multiple
> > word-processor formats foisted off on me by others.
...
> As far as I can tell, even M$-Word does not provide this. This whole
> concept seems to run counter to the exclusive access demanded by such
> programs...

MSWord does do this, quite well:  just double-click on that little
black box over the scrollbar (or pull down the "windows" menu, iirc)

This is the kind of basic capability that the Model-View-Controller
design pattern is great at providing:  for each document, you have
one "model" (the document itself) supporting multiple views, each 
with one (as in WORD) or more controllers.

Most programming editors support this property (I *think* MS notepad
does; I *know* nedit does.)


