That is one of the reasons Steve Jobs canned the idea of third party Apple
clone makers which was already underwear when he took over Apple. By
controlling the hardware, you have much better control over the software and
it is much easier to build a robust machine that doesn't take a gazillion
hours to setup and get working (and later , a gazillion <alt><ctrl><del>.

In the end, it saves a lot of money by reducing software development costs and
providing a stable platform for everyone to build on.  Apple has preserved
much of the original MAC compatibility. I still have a few apps that ran on a
MAC Plus circa 1986. Can't say that for much Intel-based software.

And note that since the original MAC came out, Apple changed its CPU platform.
Yet, 69k apps continue to run on the new platform.

Note that the MACos is far from VMS in terms of reliability. 

When on the phone with the cable-modem folks, they asked that I shutdown my
VAXes ("what's a VAX ? they asked, I said it was like a serious Unix box). I
told them "Sorry, I don't shut these machines down unless there is a power
failure" - the folks at the other end are not yet used to machines that stay
up all the time in a home.



