In comp.org.decus Ben Myers <benmyers@ultranet.com> wrote:
> Why does Windows 95/98 insist on repetitively rewriting configuration
> information to the system BIOS chip, wearing it out after a few
> thousand rewrites?  Windows 95/98 has rendered a lot of (name brand,
> too, as in mfd. by Intel) motherboards totally useless by doing so.

  Can you cite examples of this? There are two non-volatile memory areas
on the typical PC motherboard - the BIOS and the configuration NVRAM. The
BIOS is normally a flash chip with a finite number of write cycles (but
this is up in the 10K-and-up range these days) and which isn't writable
on a byte-by-byte basis, and then there's the configuration NVRAM, which
is usually in a Dallas Semiconductor (or equivalent) battery-backed RAM
chip, which has unlimited writes and is byte-rewritable.

  I'm aware of a number of motherboards that either have a flash BIOS
write protect jumper or a unique-to-the-motherboard I/O register needed
to enable writing, neither of which Windows 9x can do anything about.

  Most Intel motherboards with soldered-on flash BIOS chips have a pro-
tected area which can be used to recover from a bad flash - they auto-
boot a floppy. Most of the quality non-Intel motherboards I've seen use
socketed flash BIOS chips, so at worst the chip could be changed if an
error in flashing happened. Granted, this is inconvenient, but it doesn't
render the motherboard junk as you assert.

  Microsoft has committed a number of atrocities in Windows, but I don't
see this as one of them.

	Terry Kennedy             http://www.tmk.com
        terry@tmk.com             Jersey City, NJ USA



