In comp.org.decus Ben Myers <benmyers@ultranet.com> wrote:
> I've done a serious amount of NVRAM troubleshooting with Intel-made
> boards including the Zappa, Aladdin, Endeavor, Thor, and Morrison.
> Boards and systems arrive here, and people on the net, mostly in the
> Gateway newsgroup, describe these problems.  I give them advice to
> solve the problem.

  Your reply addresses NVRAM corruption issues. Your original post said:

>>> Why does Windows 95/98 insist on repetitively rewriting configuration
>>> information to the system BIOS chip, wearing it out after a few
>>> thousand rewrites?  Windows 95/98 has rendered a lot of (name brand,
>>> too, as in mfd. by Intel) motherboards totally useless by doing so.

  stating that Windows was damaging the BIOS chip, a different component.

> Problem #1: The built-in hard disk controller can no longer recognize
> the hard drive(s).  This one is not catastrophic.
>
> Solution #1: The NVRAM has been hosed by Windows 95.  Download
> CLRESCSD.EXE from the Gateway web site and follow the instructions for
> creating a bootable floppy diskette.  Boot from floppy, clear the
> NVRAM, and the system begins to recognize all the IDE devices again.
> Intel is somewhat culpable in this scenario, because the did not
> implement a NVRAM clear function in the BIOS setup.  One of their
> one-time competitors, Micronics, did.  

  Every time I've encountered a user with this problem, they either loaded
an IDE driver update (one of the PIIX updates, or a 3rd-party bus master
driver) or they tried playing with the beta APM support in some Win95 re-
leases. That's not to say that there aren't other reasons, but these are
the ones I've seen.

  The PC9x, DMI, and ECSD specs define the layout of the NVRAM specific-
ally so operating systems can manipulate data stored in it. Intel is a
major player in these specs, so it isn't a case of them not knowing about
them.

> Problem #2: The motherboard no longer is able to save the CMOS
> settings.  POST emits messages to the effect that the NVRAM is
> corrupted.  Windows 95 has updated, and updated and updated NVRAM,
> whether it needs to or not.  
>
> Solution #2: The NVRAM on the board is hosed.  Replace the board.
> I've seen this one first hand with both Intel-made boards and
> Micronics boards.

  You're asserting that Windows "wears out" the NVRAM? Consider the Dallas
Semiconductor DS12887 Timekeeper SRAM, a very common part motherboards of
the era you're talking about. It has 128 bytes of SRAM, a battery, and
clock circuitry. 113 bytes of SRAM are available for general purpose use,
while 15 bytes are dedicated to timekeeping functions. All 128 bytes are on
the same IC substate. The data sheet for this part - see:
http://www.dalsemi.com/DocControl/PDFs/12c887.pdf
states that that the time data in RAM is updated once per second (see the
section entitled "UPDATE CYCLE" starting at the bottom of page 7 of the
data sheet. So one byte (seconds) of the NVRAM is rewritten every second,
and another is rewritten every minute. But the NVRAM doesn't "wear out".
Also, on page 11 of that spec sheet in the section "ABSOLUTE MAXIMUM
RATINGS", there are no maximums for number of writes, write timing, etc.
so there should be no way to physically damage the chip from software -
the only notes are for overvoltage and overtemperature conditions. And I
have never seen an app note or errata from Dallas Semiconductor regarding
damage from software.

  On more modern boards like the Intel SE440BX-2, the PIIX chip includes
the NVRAM functionality. The description is very similar to the Dallas
part (which makes sense, since they are both derived from the original
PC AT spec), and can be found at:
http://developer.intel.com/design/chipsets/datashts/29056201.pdf
Section 8.9.2 is "RTC UPDATE CYCLE" and contains a description that is
very similar to the Dallas data sheet. This is on page 188 of the doc-
ument.

  It's certainly possible that something is corrupting the NVRAM, but it
should not require junking the motherboard to recover. It's certainly
possible to put a config in NVRAM that will result in the board failing
to boot, but there should be some way of clearing the NVRAM provided
(for a soldered-in part, a removable battery; for socketed parts, per-
haps just swapping the part - or better yet, reprogramming it with the
same data as found in an identical part). Some motherboards implement
jumpers to clear the NVRAM (Tyan does this).

  Intel changed from the AMI BIOS (in their older boards like the Plato)
to the Phoenix BIOS. I don't particularly like the Phoenix BIOS and I
haven't been keeping track of whether Intel provided a "clear CMOS"
option in their older Phoenix boards. Their current boards (like the
aforementioned SE440BX-2) do offer this, including a complete BIOS and
config recovery that uses a protected BIOS boot block and completely
ignores the current contents of the NVRAM. See:
http://developer.intel.com/design/motherbd/se2/se2_confg.htm

  On the motherboards you say have "hosed" NVRAM, what chips (vendor and
part number) are used to implement the NVRAM?

	Terry Kennedy             http://www.tmk.com
        terry@tmk.com             Jersey City, NJ USA



