On 22 Dec 99 11:40:44 -0700, dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett)
wrote:


>  OK, now I'm really confused. Is it the intent that the
>"Enthusiast license" can be used for classroom education? The clause
>quoted above would appear to say not ( assuming that the students
>had to pay or otherwise compensate the institution to take the class).
>i.e. College buys software, student signs up for programming class
>(and pays fee), student uses software as part of course work - it
>seems obvious that a 3rd party ( the student ) has paid the college
>a fee and has had work performed for him by this software
>( compiling his assignment ) and has therefore violated this license.
>
>  If that interpretation is correct then the "Enthusiast License" is
>of no value to an educational institution.

The software may be used, for free, in a classroom situation.  It's
even ok if the instructor obtains the software and installs it on the
classroom PCs for the students' use.  The instructor may use the
software herself in matters relating to the coursework.

We're going back and forth with the corporate lawyers on the exact
license wording.   But our intent is that use of the compiler for
classwork is covered under the free license.  What is not covered is
using the compiler as part of a "business operation", which includes
administration of an educational institution.


