David A Froble wrote:
> 
> At one time what could be done on a Cray could not be done in any reasonable
> manner on 'normal' computers.  The gap is not so large now, and that was my
> point.  It may not be weather, but there probably are some "problems" out there
> that could benefit from the giant ox.  The 'ox' that can produce an answer 100
> times faster than say, a 128 CPU Wildfire system.  Hey, as I said, it's not my
> field of expertise, and maybe the day when such is possible is past.  

I have monitored these issues for a long time, and the set of "problems"
that need this particular "ox" has been steadily shrinking.

At this point, there is pretty much only one application area where 
the vector machines can offer significantly better performance than
a system of equivalent cost made of commodity parts.   This area is,
unfortunately, in the realm of the extraordinarily highly classified,
so those of us who design systems have to take the work of the three-
letter agencies that the codes cannot be redesigned to work adequately
on commodity systems.

The total amount of money available to vendors in that market is
nowhere near large enough to support the ongoing development of a
general-purpose line of computers.



