  I'm not sure where this thread started, or where it's going, or what the 
  underlying question is...  (But since when has that ever stopped me from 
  taking an opportunity for pontification...  Or as some might say, "rising 
  to a troll"...  :-)

In article <3869413F.3E238258@us.oracle.com>, norm lastovica <nlastovi@us.oracle.com> writes:
:certainly there are restrictions around things
:like record sizes (these are well spelled out
:in the Rdb documentation).  Some of this relates
:all the way back to string descriptors with
:16 bit length fields.  Additionally, there are
:other length restrictions to take into accout
:(255 byte file specifications for one).  But
:32 bit pointers have always been there.  The
:documented data structures in memory and on disk
:remain as they always have.

  RMS has limitations based on 8, 16, 24, 32, and 64 bit-width fields...

  RMS has been fully native (32-bit address space) since at least V3.0...
  (My recollection on V1 and V2 is fuzzy, and I'm not particularly inclined
  to go dig through the old kits to look for this arcana right now...)

  Various file system data structures -- the various caches, obviously -- 
  have been moving into 64-bit space on OpenVMS Alpha...

  The older quadword-derived "string descriptors" scattered among the 
  RMS data structures and the word-length record size fields found in 
  various on-disk record structures are among the commonly-encountered 
  limits within the RMS file system...

  nb: Filename specifications can now be upwards of 236 characters in 
  length (118 for wide characters) and up to 255 directories deep, with 
  the advent of the RMS NAML structure and the ODS-5 FI5DEF-related 
  support... This stuff first appeared in OpenVMS Alpha V7.2...

  As has been stated elsewhere, we are looking at a new file system to 
  account for what we've learned from ODS-1 through ODS-5 as well as from
  Spiralog ("Dollar") and from various other file and database systems, 
  as well as from where we expect the future file system requirements to 
  be going, and we have A/D work underway here in OpenVMS Engineering...

  What was the question again?  (What I see for a question here is quite
  ambiguous, as are most discussions of "bit-ness"; most discussions of
  "bit-ness" found outside of marketing literature... :-)

