mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote:
> 

> On the down side, for Compaq's bottom line, the availability of these
> compilers at this price probably spells the end of Tru64 as a number
> cruncher in academic and many industrial settings.  Tru64 is slightly more
> stable than Linux but that's barely an issue in this market and now that
> the performance edge is gone, there's not much reason to spend extra for
> an otherwise very similar OS.  I'll estimate there will be at least a 40%
> attrition in Tru64 systems in the CSLG over the next year (due to
> conversion to Linux) and an even steeper decline (to approximately zero) of
> sales of Tru64 workstations into academic sites.  

I severely doubt you will see anything like the attrition rate you are
describing in academic sites.  The people running Tru64 or Digital Unix
are already entrenched and aren't going to just jump ship for Linux.
For a couple dollars.  It would have to be thousands before you see
any significant shift.  The price differential you describe for Tru64
and Linux licensing isn't very large.  Under the CSLG program I was 
licensing the Tru64 OS *and* compilers for $50 per machine (less than
the cost of licensing the Linux version of the compiler). Managers of
large numbers of systems, quite frankly, don't want to be bothered with
the hassle of managing (or switching) to another OS unless there are
*big* reasons for the switch.

I've spent time managing Digital Unix for Alphas and Intel/Linux
machines.
There is a big difference.  With Digital Unix everything worked right
out
of the box and the machines were stable from day one.  With Linux
everytime
I build a machine with a new configuration I have to be ready for the
inevitable week of OS debugging to get the machine stable.  Now once
you've
got patch.x.x.x and the right kernel I've found Linux to be quite
stable,
it's just getting to that point that's a pain.  The only reason why I
put
up with the Intel/Linux hassle now is because I can build dual CPU nodes
for about $700 each.

Jeff



