Many times what you are seeing is the dumping of a spare parts kits, or
parts salvaged from dead units.

Some of the equipment ends up dumped from a Bankruptcy or foreclosure sale.

Also the disposition policies of some firms are rather silly.  It is almost
impossible to sell the part of the unit that has the "brass tag" on it, but
it can be broken up in pieces.  Then they PAY a scrap dealer to take it
away.


There is also a high implied suspicion that the Employee trying to get the
asset sold may have a vested interest in the buyer, and that is against the
ethics clause.  After all that equipment cost over $20,000 new just a few
years ago, and is now only worth a few hundred dollars.  (It must be a scam
to skim money from the corporation.)


Donations are also difficult for the same reason.  There is usually a
special committee to decide who will get donation.  Targeted donations are
not permitted and are viewed as in the previous paragraph.  So donations are
only considered from the contacts of the committee, who has absolutely no
contact with anyone that has a clue of what surplus equipment is available
or what non-profit institutions can use them.  All they know about is what
has been requested to them, usually again by people that do not have any
knowledge of computers, so only slightly used office equipment is usually
donated this way.


So once a piece of equipment is marked surplus, and the manager needs the
space, he wants it gone as soon as possible.  The time and effort that it
takes to either sell or donate the equipment is prohibited and can even be
career limiting.

Since they have procedures for disposal of hazardous waste, they PAY to have
the hauled away to a scrap dealer.


The scrap dealer then asks around until he can find someone to assess the
parts, where they end up on the surplus market.  There is one such fellow
about 20 miles south of here.  If he can not identify the parts, he cuts all
of the gold plated parts off, and salvages what else he can.

-John
Not representing anyone





