Kenn Humborg <kenn@avalon.no46.wombat.ie> wrote in message
news:slrn85b1uc.os9.kenn@avalon.no46.wombat.ie...
> On 13 Dec 1999 22:34:36 GMT, Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam>
wrote:
> >    Spin down the RA90 or RA92 drive...
> >    Take both A and B ports offline...
>
> I'm curious about something.  My VAXstation 3500 (with zero
> documentation) has an RA70 disk.  This disk has two connectors
> marked Port A and Port B.  Port A is connected to the KDA50
> controller and Port B goes to a sort of 'connector cage' at
> the left end of the drive area (BA213 enclosure).
>
> Is this port B intended for sharing the disk among two
> machines, cluster-like?

Not exactly.  Only port A or port B may be active at a time, and the
transition delay is longer than what you would want for a cluster.

It can be used to connect the disk to another SDI controller for fault
tolerance in case of a controller failure, and works very well for DATA
disks.  Because only one path is active at a time, it will not work for a
SYSTEM disk to be shared between two Q-BUS systems.

Nor is is suitable for a quorum disk.

If you were to connect the disk to two hosts, you would likely also want to
use shadowing as disk failures are far more frequent than controler
failures.

> Do the 'A0' and 'B0' buttons on the
> front panel take the disk off-line for each machine
> individually?

Yes.

You can also wire the connector cage to allow you to add external RA90
series disks to your system.  The KDA50 controller will support 4 disks.
IIRC: You can add disks while the system is running.

-John
