> :In reply to myself and, collectively, all those who've replied to my
> :original posting: thanks!
> :   Oh, BTW...  Hoff, my problem with the serial terminal isn't so much
the
> :DB9 pinout -- I have
> :the official DEC cable for that -- as not knowing whether the pinout on
the
> :DB25 end is compatible with the C.Itoh terminal I'm using ...
> :whether the cable "ought" to be "straight through" vs. "swapped"...
>
>   Many years ago, I had occasion to use C.Itoh terminals in place of the
>   then-current VT100 and VT220 series terminals.  I do not remember if the
>   necessary pinout was cross-over modem vs straight through, but I know
>   the C.Itoh terminals would work.

All terminals and computers should be DTE devices, and require a cross-over
modem cable when connected directly.  The gender of the connector on the
terminal does not matter in this case.

All C.Itoh terminals that I have seen have used the same wiring as the
Digital models.

Some third party terminals may require special and strange jumpering
configurations on the connector on the terminal side before they will
function.

To test a terminal device, jumper pins 2 and 3 together and type.  If you do
not see anything, jumper pins 4 and 5 together.

If that still does not produce output, then jumper 6 and 20 together.  Pin 8
may need to be jumpered to 20 but usually can be ignored.


If these do not work, your terminal is either broken, or you have a very
strange one, like the WYSE I picked up at a surplus auction for $1.00 U.S.
It requires some normally unused pins around pins around 20 to be jumpered,
or it refuses to work

-John
