In article <uYT84.67$4c.254573@news.optonline.net>, 
    	"Frank Sapienza" <sapienza@noesys.com> writes:
> Simple question (yeah, right):
> 
> In a cluster using DSSI for the interconnect, if there are two shared DSSI
> buses, does SCS use only one or both buses for cluster communications?  If
> it uses only one of them, how does it select which one to use?

        SCS  will  use  _all_  available  interconnects  and dynamically
    switch  from  one path to another based on what it thinks  the  best
    response/throughput will be.  So it  will  use  both  of  your  DSSI
    busses, plus Ethernet or whatever other interconnects are available.

        Note that SCS sometimes makes  mistakes, for example, where both
    FDDI and Ethernet are available.  If the FDDI is heavily loaded, SCS
    may  switch  to  the  lower  capacity  Ethernet...temporarily...thus
    exacerbating the load problem.  Work is going on  in  this  area  to
    properly  account for the capacity of the different network devices.
    Hopefully we'll see those  changes  in the next, as-yet-unannounced,
    version of VMS.

        -Ken
