MSchell wrote:
> 
> I disagree on the naming.  Both CompacTape I and II are still DLT tapes and
> drives, just very early versions.  I think the TZ30 and TZ50 were both
> 15/30, or even 10/20 DLT drives.

Ha!  Try "0.090" and "0.270" if you're referring to capacity of the
TK50 and TK70 carts.  10 Gigabytes is off by a factor of 100 or so.

And there is a fundamental difference between the TK50/TK70 physical
cartridge and the DLT cartridge: You *cannot* put a DLT cartridge
in a TK50 or TK70 drive, it's physically prevented by the presence
of a raised notch on the left side of the tape cartridge.

Yes, they're all Linear Tape cartridges designed by Digital, but
they aren't all DLT's.

> CompacTape refers to the brand name of the tape manufacturer, which was
> originally 3M.

I'm not disagreeing about the actual manufacturer, but CompacTape
was a trademark of Digital.  If you don't believe me, you can come
visit my archive of several thousand TK50 carts, each saying
"CompacTape is a trademark of Digital Equipment Corporation" on
the enclosed handling instructions.

