Yes, but given the market penetration of MS Office, its file formats and
other associated data interchange methods constitute a de facto standard.
MS is working very hard to leverage this into both new and repeat sales of
its product, and to reduce the competition into oblivion. I have yet to see,
for instance, a non-PowerPoint application to display even fairly simple 
PowerPoint presentations properly - often, seemingly trivial things such as
font size selection and text positioning are wrong.

One of the remedies resulting from the current anti-trust trial should in
my opinion be that such file formats should be documented in detail (possibly
including a reference viewer application that would be in the public domain),
with some suitable mechanism in place to ensure that any changes made do not
gratuitously break existing code and interoperability.

