From: mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Subject: Linux/Alpha compilers - and the price is right
Date: 1999/12/22
Message-ID: <83r8d2$fba@gap.cco.caltech.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 563902061
Organization: Biology Division, Caltech, Pasadena CA 91125
Reply-To: mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms

It looks like Compaq did the right thing for a change!

Compaq has released its C and Fortran compilers for Linux/Alpha.
C++ is in the works but not yet available.  A link to information
about these compilers is at:

   http://www.unix.digital.com/linux/software.htm

Near as I can tell, the prices break down like this:

     Usage=  Educational/Hobbyist     Commercial/Institutional/Academic
  C          Free                     Free (!!!)
  Fortran    Free                     $399

It's a bit unclear with Fortran where "education" stops and "institutional"
begins, but I'm pretty sure that the license would preclude use in most
academic sites since the clause

  "Any work that is performed or produced as a result of use of this
  Software cannot be performed or produced for the benefit of other parties
  for a fee, compensation or any other reimbursement or remuneration."

would seem to rule out paying even student programmers.  On the other hand,
they sell the commercial Fortran variant in 5 and 10 packs which bring the
price per system down to $190 and $140 respectively.  That's peanuts for a
compiler like this by any accounting.  Since no mass market software that
I'm aware of is written in Fortran the (slight) price for this package is
not likely to impede the spread of Linux/Alpha. 

As far as promoting Linux/Alpha it looks like Compaq really hit the center
of the target this time.  Hopefully C++ prices will be in line with those 
for C, as gcc has both, and it is the "competition" in this instance.  C++
is also the language used for much commercial software development.
However, Alpha hardware still costs at least 2X as much as equivalent x86
systems, so they still need to take that bull by the horns to achieve
significant Linux/Alpha market penetration. 

On the down side, for Compaq's bottom line, the availability of these 
compilers at this price probably spells the end of Tru64 as a number
cruncher in academic and many industrial settings.  Tru64 is slightly more
stable than Linux but that's barely an issue in this market and now that
the performance edge is gone, there's not much reason to spend extra for
an otherwise very similar OS.  I'll estimate there will be at least a 40%
attrition in Tru64 systems in the CSLG over the next year (due to
conversion to Linux) and an even steeper decline (to approximately zero) of
sales of Tru64 workstations into academic sites.  OpenVMS is also going to
take a hit.  Heck, I would migrate my one OpenVMS system at this point
except that some of the key software is licensed only for OpenVMS so I
can't easily move it. 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



