Getting off topic, but I have to present a different opinion on the Corvair. 
What killed the Corvair was the Ford Mustang.  The Corvair was a very nice car
with a lot of balance.  The engine could get 180 HP with turbocharging and
that's about all it could handle outside of special conditions.  Such a car
wasn't what was selling in the late 1960s.  This was the era of 426 hemi-head
cryslers, 427 Ford and Chevy monster engines, real serious muscle cars, I could
probably name dozens, the like of which hasn't been seen since, and probably
never will again, what with federal regs and such.  The Mustang had the V-8
engine and would smoke the Corvair on every green light, and that's what sold in
them years.  Ah, what a glorious time to drive.  I had a 1969 Z-28 Camaro, one
of the originals that came stock with the tuned headers, and then had a bit of
balancing and porting work done.  Never met a car on the road that could could
touch it, that is, when I could afford to keep it in appropriate rear tires. :-(

