I won't try to argue that the Corvair had the potential to be anything more
than a niche player (in addition to economical basic transportation, of
course - which wasn't something to sneeze at even before the oil crunches) -
but the niche it occupied was conspicuously empty, even of European entries,
and those who appreciated it did so vehemently.  After the 1965 body change,
it was also about the easiest car to look at in anything near its price
range for the 1950 - 1980 time period - in fact, it would still look fairly
contemporary today.

My father had a '65 Olds 442, and I had my share of interesting acceleration
experiences with it.  But my preference (when not out cruising) was for my
own '67 Corvair, which with Konis and Pirellis on all four corners was (as I
intimated earlier) the closest thing to a 911 I was likely to own for -
well, given that 911 prices have always kept well ahead of any reasonable
vehicle expenditure I was likely to make - ever.

Then again, I was more into rallying than dragging, at least as far as
formal competition went.  And I was fortunate enough to navigate for some
years in an ex-factory 911-R rally car a friend owned - which was *almost*
as fast in a straight line as the 442 (which had long enough gearing - a
specially-ordered 2.72 rear axle ratio with a wide-ratio (Ford!) 3-speed
Hurst floor shift that would just hit 60 in first gear - that it would
likely have come close to its theoretical top speed of around 140 if we'd
ever had tires on it that I trusted anywhere near there).

We now return you to your regularly-scheduled material.

- bill

