David A Froble <davef@tsoft-inc.com> writes:

> So, what are you saying here?  Market penetration?  Isn't that what every
> product strives for?  And if you succeed, then what?  People call your 
> product a de facto standard, and want to take it away from you?  Sure as
> hell doesn't sound like Capitalism to me.

The point is that pure capitalism's fixed point is a situation where only a
single "competitor" (no longer having competition) survives*, and from that
point on can dictate her conditions. As an example, see the changes brought
about recently in Intel's products, prices and roadmap by the appearance
(finally!) of AMD with a competitive product: Do you think you could buy a
PIII-700 (if you can find one...) if the Athlon wasn't available?

It's not about taking away, it's about restricting their freedom of action in
defined ways.

> Too bad there's never been a de facto standard of when a company
> should reign in its success.

That standard is written into the anti-trust laws and the associated case law.

