Huh? If I review the sentence I wrote (above), I see nothing about cause and
effect re: AlphaNT customers.

> The
> reason NT sales went down over that time period was that it was clear
> to developers and large customers (unfortunately not home users) that
> Digital and Microsoft were abandoning the platform, and after this
> corporate strategy had succeeded in reducing volumes to the electron
> microscopic quantities you quote, the formal announcement was made.
> Certainly NT sales could have been quite a major factor if the
> principals involved had chosen to make it so.
>

January 1998 happened to be the time that Compaq announced its intentions to
buy Digital. Sales began to tank immediately thereafter.

I really don't think Digital would have entertained any intention of
dropping the platform if volumes and revenue continued to increase or at
least remained stable.

Same can be said for Micro$oft.

Compaq pulled the plug when the effort became prohibitively expensive. The
write-up I did several months ago (SKC V6N25, available at www.acersoft.com)
significantly understated the financial losses the Q was experiencing during
the Last Days of AlphaNT.

The AlphaNT effort was doomed right from the get-go due to the fact that
Digital got the short (and quite sh*tty) end of the stick in the Alliance
for Enterprise Computing pact it signed with Micro$oft. Had Digital's
negotiators not abdicated their responsibility to due diligence on behalf of
their stockholders, DEC would not have settled for the first deal Micro$oft
threw over the transom.

As I'm sure you know, the A.E.C. was the "agreement" DEC and Micro$oft
reached after DEC threatened Micro$oft with legal action over Micro$oft's
misuse of DEC intellectual property (the MICA OS) in Windows NT. DEC never
was a very litigious beast, and avoiding entanglements that benefit only
lawyers usually is the best course of action. That said, DEC got a "deal"
which included faint praise for Alpha by Micro$oft, Micro$oft's agreement to
port NT server apps to Alpha concurrent with Intel, and very little else.

Had DEC demanded Alpha-Intel CLIENT apps parity, the AlphaNT apps base would
have been much larger. Apps availability is critical to a platform's
survival, and the portfolio of native AlphaNT apps never amounted to much
(it stabilized at ~3K several years ago IIRC).

Even after accepting an utterly squatulent deal from Micro$oft, DEC tried to
make a go of things. Remember the Celebris XL "Dream Machine," the first
NT-specific platform from any vendor? And then the lobotomized NT-only
"beige box" Alphas? And all the advertising (those silly monkeys, etc) DEC
did to promote NT on Alpha?

It wasn't DEC who betrayed AlphaNT customers, because DEC ceased to exist
more than 14 months before the plug was pulled on AlphaNT. I can't speak for
Compaq, but I think it came down to a Hobson's Choice: Who do you betray?
Some of your customers or all of your stockholders?

Whatever, the debacle serves as an object lesson of what invariably happens
to vendors who make Faustian pacts with Bill Gates.

terry shannon










