"James Van Buskirk" <torsop@ix.netcom.com> wrote in message
news:83k2ap$4li$1@nntp3.atl.mindspring.net...
>
> Terry C. Shannon wrote in message ...
>
> >It wasn't DEC who betrayed AlphaNT customers, because DEC ceased to exist
> >more than 14 months before the plug was pulled on AlphaNT. I can't speak
> for
> >Compaq, but I think it came down to a Hobson's Choice: Who do you betray?
> >Some of your customers or all of your stockholders?
>
>
> This paragraph seems to sum up best your complete misinterpretation of
> my post.  DEC never really had their hearts in the Alpha NT project.  In
> another paragraph you refer to DEC's advertising of Alpha-NT but even
> though I saw about 10 DEC ads for every Intel ad in that time frame, I
> don't recall ever seeing one that mentioned Alpha-NT.  Maybe I just never
> watch the right shows on the idiot box.  I would say that easily 95% of
> the home computer buying public never heard of Alpha, NT or otherwise.
> Since nobody at DEC ever heard of the law of supply and demand (what was
> the marginal cost of producing an Alpha, anyway?) they had the best chip
> out there by far (even current x86 machines aren't as fast as my 2 year
> old Alpha, at least for the things I make it do), they priced it so high
> that no home user could afford one and as a consequence never were able
> to sell even 30% of their fab's capacity.

Back in 1991 when DEC put together its original marketing plan, the firm
intended to go head-to-head with Intel. A ~$3K Alpha PC (based on the 21068
ULC-Alpha chip) was on the product roadmap for early release.

A decision to scuttle the "Triumph" box was made because the marketeers
neglected to consider what OS and what apps this "Intel-killer" would run.
Ironically, the decision to build the Fab-6 plant in Hudson was predicated
on ROI figures that took into consideration ridiculously high Alpha
volumes... volumes that only could have accrued from a low-end product.

With the low-end product cancelled even before ground was broken for
Palmer's Folly, it's a wonder the beancounters didn't run the numbers again.
Had they done so, they would have drawn the same conclusion you did above!


> Their belief in an ever-
> increasing high-end market was completely contrary to the ordinary
> consumer's expectation that as technology advances one should be able
> to get better hi-tech devices at lower price points as time goes on.
> You blame Microsoft almost exclusively for trashing Alpha NT, but both
> parties needed to take steps (Digital in terms of production, marketing,
> and pricing, and Microsoft needed to do more development first on Alpha,
> which would make sense because development tends to go faster on a
> faster machine) and the fact that Microsoft didn't doesn't excuse DEC
> from not following through on the steps it would have been necessary
> for them to have made to create a viable windows RISC-PC platform.

Correct on the cost factor... DEC never "got it" WRT commodity hardware
price points. But without the software for the consumer marketplace, the
hardware had no chance of succeeding. I don't blame Microsoft for much of
anything... it takes two parties to make a deal, and DEC settled for a
**really** lousy deal. Without NT client apps on Alpha, the platform didn't
stand a chance in the consumer marketplace and hence was doomed to low
market penetration.







