In article <FMzuut.8yB@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>If Samsung is
>unhappy about anything, it's the fact that IBM will come on board as a
>fabber in the EV7 era. Since IBM is well ahead of Samsung in process
>technology (copper, et al), Samsung pretty much was forced to invest the
                                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>~$200M that was discussed during last Monday's announcement.

That's pretty much my view of the whole announcement.  These are the kinds
of routine expenses that keeping a high tech product like the Alpha afloat
require.  It was kind of like replacing the brake pads and clutch on a car
- sure it's an expense, but the only other choice is to junk the vehicle. 
There was nothing "extra" in it other than,  maybe, the $100M that they are
spending pushing Tru64.  Most companies would consider advertising for a
product a routine expense but historically that was never the case with
Digital products!  In any case, Compaq is trying to get double mileage out
of the Tru64 announcement - they announced that expense separately and then
included it again in the total for the present announcement. 

I wonder how much of the $100 Million for Tru64 is going towards
subsidizing all of those OpenVMS "seeds" packages they are shipping to
people who send in their $100 for the noncommercial Tru64 program? Yes,
strange as it may seem, many people report that they obtained about $1500
worth of _OpenVMS_ materials (including the full 4Q99 software and
documentation libraries), without requesting any of it, when they purchased
noncommercial _Tru64_.  Now we all know that $829 for QA-5FX8A-A8 (software
library with OS) is about $800 of pure profit, but you gotta wonder what
Compaq's books must look when they send these out in droves along with the
those Tru64 boxes.

