Well, not ENTIRELY useless, but could be more correct, and CERTAINLY
more positive! I quote from your web page...

> You'll most likely want to get the vertical stand for it, and stand it 
> upright on its side in order to allow the heat to escape.

As John Malmberg points out at 
http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/hobbyist/mltianot.html#heat
the stand is an integral part of the cooling that the unit requires.

> And, if you have the means, take out the fan that's already in there 
> blowing on the CPU from the side, and install a more powerful one.

Yes - an excellent idea. Highly recommendable field modification to the
UDB.

> If you put a 3.5" HDD inside (how you'll get it to fit, or how you'll 
> power it is beyond me), ...

The Multia only accomdates 2-1/2 inch hard disks. Cooling an internal
disk does add significantly to the existing heat problems. Power
connections should be available, or you may need to improvise, depending
upon how the unit was configured when it shipped from Digital.

> Nowadays, CompaQ offers Tru64 UNIX package at $99 for non-commercial
> use.

...and the DFWCUG (on behalf of Compaq) provides the OpenVMS Hobbyist
program at little cost (only your Internet connection and e-mail
expenses). See http://www.montagar.com/hobbyist/

Additional OpenVMS hobbyist support:
http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/

> You probably also figured out that Multias are completely worthless for 
> any real use. It doesn't matter, though, because you probably already 
> overpaid for one on eBay.

Sadly, both points are true. The Multia is useful for the OpenVMS
(Tru64, Linux, *BSD, etc.) hobbyist, but not for any really serious
work. You can usually find these cheaper from other sources.

Then again, it might make a fair firewall, proxy server, e-mail gateway,
etc. for a SOHO application.

