Robert DiRosario wrote:
> 
> How do I attach a keyboard and mouse to a Personal DECstation 5000/25?
> I just picked one up but didn't get any cables with it.  I have a
> DECstation 5000/240 and a VAXstation 3100/M48, but they use different
> cables for the keyboard and mouse.  On the back is a connector that
> looks like it will take an RJ-45 plug.  Do I take a standard DEC
> keyboard and mouse and plug them into an adapter that then plugs into
> the RJ-45 connector?

The connector is actually not a RJ-45 but something called Serial.BUS.
It works a bit like on a Mac, where you connect the keyboard to the
machine and the mouse to the keyboard.
The cables can be hard to find, but if you're lucky you can either find
them somewhere or maybe even buy them as spare parts from Compaq.

...but since you were able to get hands on the keyboard & mouse, do
whatever you can to get hands on the cables too. They are quite
important.
 
> Do I need to have the keyboard and mouse attached for the system to
> boot?  If I attach a monitor to the video connector on the system board
> and turn the system on all I get is a small white square in the lower
> left part of the display.  If I connect it to the video card above the
> system board the display is blank.

The 5000/25 is made so that it'll detect if you have a keyboard
connected to the S.BUS. If you have it'll turn on the graphics adapter,
but if you don't, it'll use the serial port instead.
This port uses a MMJ connector (the one that looks like a enhanced RJ-11
connector), and runs usually at 9600-N-8-1. These connectors are more
common, and used on all DEC equipment since the early VAX days or so.

BTW: It seems you could be interested in the PDF file I have entitled
"Personal DECstation 5000 - Hardware Maitenance Guide" ?


