Fired up the 3100 that I picked up at auction last year, since dragged
it half way across Australia, finally got the inspiration to try and get
it net-booting again. Plugged the monitor power cord into the back of
the workstation (first time I think I've done this), powered it up.
Seemed to be working, saw the graphics test sequences on the monitor,
was a bit curious about what the 'tic-tic-tic' noise was coming out of
the workstation. Finally dawned on me that it wasn't the floppy drive
making that noise, realised it sounded like the PSU arcing over. Just
got around to bending my head down towards the left side of the case to
take a closer look/listen, then bang/flash. Uh oh.

Well, it's not easy getting that thing (the PSU) apart (do you really
have to get the PSU's board out to replace the fuses? I mean, you could
possibly do it leaving the board in, but it looks like a bit of a
hit-and-miss affair to do it!).

Anyhow, couldn't see any obvious blown components or carbon traces from
any major arcing, but the fuse for the PSU is blown (the other one
appears to be for the outgoing mains power connector). Now, these fuses
appear to be 240V/5A (5 AMP! For the PSU! Isn't it rated at around 150W
or something?)

Q: Does anyone know the specs for these fuses? And where I could get
them in Australia?

I'm going to give that board (and the rest of the system) a good dusting
tomorrow. I can almost believe that there's enough dust there and that
it was sufficiently humid tonight to allow some HV arcing (although I'm
not anywhere nearly as sure about that being responsible for blowing a
5A fuse!). Anyway, I'd like to make sure I put the right sort of fuse
back in. Just in case. Then I just have to keep hoping that those
electrons didn't manage to get to where they're not supposed to be.


Thanks,

Wayne


