In article <839aac$7n6$1@hecate.umd.edu>,
David T. Wang <davewang@wam.umd.edu@club_HYK@Glue.umd.edu> wrote:
>Jeffrey J. Potoff (potoff@chem.umn.edu) wrote:

>: I do remember some folks as UMD having bizzarely equiped DEC 3000
>: where the video card was no longer supported under DU4.0d.  This
>: is complicated by the fact that they aren't PCI based machines,
>: so your video card choices are limited and rather pricey unless
>: you can pick up something used.
>
>: As someone else mentioned, Linux may be another option, and not a 
>: bad one at that.

Nope. Linux is not a good option for Turbochannel machines of any
kind, with the possible exception of the Personal Decstation.
(Even there, NetBSD is better.)

>These things have Turbo Channel or soemthing like that, and as far as I
>know, Linux doesn't work.  We have gotten NetBSD to work, but as I 
>mentioned in the earlier post, failed thus far to get the framebuffer to 
>work properly.  

That sounds unlikely. The Personal Decstations (MAXINE motherboard)
only have two 12.5MHz slots and have an onboard video subsystem.  The
onboard video is definitely supported in NetBSD, so if you have a
1024x768 sync-on-green (or greyscale) monitor, you can always use that.

It's just possible these systems came with an add-on turbochannel
graphics card.  NetBSD 1.4.x has support for all the `dumb'
turbochannel graphics card.  It does not have support for the PMAG-C
(older 2d accelerated) card, but -current does have support
for the PMAG-C and PMAG-D/E.  

The PMAG-F is three slots, so you cannot physically get one
into a Personal DECstation.

If you have some other kind of card (PMAG-J truecolour!?), I'd be very
interested in getting one to work on NetBSD support.


>It seems no matter which OS we use, the frame buffer support will be 
>the key.  

True, but NetBSD 1.4.x really does support the cfb (PMAG-B), sfb
(PMAGB-BA), and mfb (PMAG-A?) cards, and last week's NetBSD-current
snapshot supports the PMAG-C and D/E as well.



