One need only recall the conditions here: Intel was forced to agree
to fab Alphas for n years (where the rumor was n=7). Intel now
finds itself trying to push a competing technology (Merced/Itanium)
which has been reported to be embarrassingly slow (recent Register
article) and at any rate it must view Alpha as a major competitor
to its future 64 bit systems.

Then ask: can Intel be expected to be aggressive about moving Alpha
to 18 micron processes, pushing its yield of same, in short honoring
its word, where it believes it is cutting its own throat in so doing,
or can it be expected to delay, obfuscate, propose Alpha for 25 or
35 micron processes, and so on, and devote its best fab lines for
its own stuff?

If you believe they are human, you must expect they'll be strongly
tempted to the latter course. Compaq and others can then be very
well served to find other fab sites, where there will be no
temptation to rejoice in things that go wrong for Alpha.

For the product, this is also likely good: it no longer would
be fab'd anywhere within reach of possibly non-friendly hands.

Glenn Everhart

