The Personal DECstation 5000/25 has a MIPS R3000. It'll run NetBSD/pmax
without any problems... I do that at home, and everything works pretty
well, even the graphics adapter so you can run X-Windows (if you have
the right monitor).

I used to run OpenBSD/pmax on it, but changed to NetBSD due to the
better support & faster development of NetBSD. The installation is also
very much plug-n-play, especially if you're able to dd the simple
filesystem to a disk on another unix machine.
The good thing with NetBSD/pmax is that allmost everything works, where
OpenBSD/pmax still has quite alot of sharp edges.


Regarding the DEC3000s, we have lots of them at work, also with 64 MB
RAM. They'll run OpenBSD/alpha fine, and I think I've had NetBSD/alpha
on one of them at some time too. FreeBSD/alpha is still pretty new, and
I'm not sure it likes TurboChannel.
Linux/alpha for sure doesn't like TurboChannel, and I haven't even
bothered trying to install Linux on DEC3000. I only use Linux on new PCI
based alpha's.
The DEC3000s will, ofcourse, also run Compaq/Digital Unix, from the very
old versions until today (5.0). Just remember to have the latest
firmware on it, and you'll be able to go from scratch to a complete
system within an hour. Installation is very easy.

And ofcourse, they'll also run OpenVMS.


....so there's actually quite alot of opportunities. The 5000s are
rather slow, but the DEC3000s are decent machines. I do most of my
software development on a DEC3000-M600 running dunix 4.0F.


