Ok, a real valid response, and the customers come off looking more than a little
bad.  I also couldn't make SD and therefore was not part of the solution.

This sounds just too simple, but it is.  If there were old locations that
provided useful information, why did they go away?  There is no valid reason for
this, NONE!  In the end, it's only disk space, and please don't try to tell me
you are lacking in this resource.  The complete Digital site, good and bad,
could be one button on the new Compaq site, and it would not hamper the
development of the Compaq site in any manner, and would have avoided all the
drubbing you've received over the degradation of the Compaq site.

If Compaq views their web site as solely a marketing tool, then regardless of
the quality of the site, it is doomed to failure.  There are always going to be
customers that are looking for useful technical information, like the SCSI ID
pins on a drive, what graphics HW works in a DS10, the maximum memory allowed on
a VAX 6000 model 210, etc.  The info may be for current offerings or very old
hardware/software.  If you fail these customers, then no amount of marketing
hype will keep them in the future.  Maybe the marketing department should have a
mandate to retain current customers first, and then obtain new customers.

I'd think that there is room in the Compaq web site for both marketing and
technical information.  Hey, maybe I'm way off base on this, I'm no web expert. 
I will suggest this.  Go to WWW.SEAGATE.COM and do a search on ST39173 and then
go into one of the items found.  Pick one with technical specs.  Look at the
info on SCSI ID pins and such.  I've never seen (doesn't mean they didn't exist)
such info on the DEC/Compaq web site(s).  This type of info should be as easy to
find on the Compaq site as it is for you to find it on the Seagate site, and I'd
say that's a minimum standard, not a cap.

