Organization: INRIA Rocquencourt, projet Cristal
Mime-Version: 1.0
NNTP-Posting-Date: 16 Dec 1999 20:11:05 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.sys.dec


Your idle Alpha cycles are needed for the 7th ECDL challenge:

     http://cristal.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl7/

ECDL stands for Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm, and is the
problem at the heart of the ECC public key cryptosystem put forward by
Certicom (http://www.certicom.com) as a credible alternative to RSA.
To assay the strength of this cryptosystem, Certicom has published a
number of ECDL problems.  The first six were solved by Irish
mathematician Robert Harley of INRIA (France). The sixth one, solved
last summer, took 40 days on a network of 750 machines.  Robert Harley
is now attacking the seventh challenge, which is one order of
magnitude harder.  About 800 machines are already working on it, and
estimated completion time is over one year.

Alpha-powered machines are by far the most productive in this effort,
because the problem is ideally suited to 64-bit architectures with
large register sets and fast integer arithmetic.  If we can get, say,
1000 recent Alphas to work on this challenge, we could finish in a
couple of months.

Unix source code and precompiled Linux binaries are available under
the GPL at the URL above.  The program uses lots of CPU time but very
little memory.  It's the ideal candidate to run as background process
on your machine! Communication is done by e-mail or HTTP to a central
site.  Note that this is not a trivially parallel exhaustive search;
some rather subtle mathematics are involved.

For more information on ECDL and on how to participate, see
the ECDL7 challenge pages, http://cristal.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl7/

Have fun and good luck!

- Xavier Leroy
