Organization: NASA Goddard Space Flight Center -- Greenbelt, Maryland USA
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: sci.electronics.repair,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.ultrix

I don't know about a 290-D3, but DEC does have a 290-DA.

Somewhere, on one of the Linux DEC Station or NetBSD DEC Station home pages, I
found that the specs for the DEC turbo-channel video cards matched the specs of
the VR19-HA.

If you look at www.monitorworld.com you will find:

DEC VR290-DA:
    Res:     1024 x 768
    V freq: 54 kHz
    H freq: 60 hz

VR19HA:
    Res:     1280 x 1024
    V freq: 77 kHz
    H freq: 72 hz

At home, I use the DEC VR19-HA on my DEC Station 5000/240,
and the VR290-DA on my VAX station 3100.

I don't think the 290-DA will work on the DEC Station.

Both monitors are fixed frequency.  Using monitors at the wrong frequency can
damage or destroy them.  (Some die in a few seconds, others may take many hours.)

You can get VGA monitors that will do sync-on-green and handle the
high refreash rate.  I have a HP 19" SVGA monitor, can't recall the model number.

It has 5 BNC connectors and a VGA to BNC cable.  I use it with a SVGA card in my
Linux box, but it also works on the DEC Station and VAX Station.  I got it used
at a ham-fest.  I'm sure it would be very expensive to buy new.

Hope this helps you.
----------------------------------------------------------
