"David T. Wang" wrote:
> 
> We've recently inherited a small pile of DEC 3000's and 5000/25's.
> I believe the 5000/25's have MIPs processors in them, and there is no
> current plan to use them.  We're likely just to send them to the University's
> recyling center.  However, we would like to see what we can do with the
> DEC 3000's.  They're all filled with 64 MB of RAM I believe, Disk size I do
> not know.  From what I have read on this newsgroup, NetBSD is about the
> only option that we have.  (The OS, manuals have long been lost)
> 
> However, our local OS guru tells me that the framebuffer in these boxes
> is not supported, so I take it that the only thing which could be done
> would be to pile them together as a small compute cluster.
> 

The DEC 3000 should be a reasonably fast Alpha based machine.  If
you have a pile of them and some free time you could build a 
small cluster of them and run DU on them.  Don't worry about
the OS.  You can license it under the CSLG program at UMD
for about $50/per machine, if I remember right.  Send an
email to softlmgr@umd5.umd.edu for more info about the CSLG
program.  There is no need to go throught the "hobbyist" program.

I do remember some folks as UMD having bizzarely equiped DEC 3000
where the video card was no longer supported under DU4.0d.  This
is complicated by the fact that they aren't PCI based machines,
so your video card choices are limited and rather pricey unless
you can pick up something used.

As someone else mentioned, Linux may be another option, and not a 
bad one at that.

Jeff




