Please pass on to Warren Sander

I understand that combining web pages from 3 plus companies into a coherent easy to use manner is a Herculean
task.  If you do it right you will receive the undying gratitude of thousands of existing and new Compaq
customers.  If you do it wrong there will be a great wailing and gnashing of teeth. Tough life but it comes
with the territory.

While Digital was terrible in marketing, they did do one good thing. The Standard and Options Catalog.  With
the SOC, one could find useful information in a relatively quick manner.  One could do a very good job of
configuring a system that had a reasonable chance of working.  The only thing that would have made life easier
would have been to had pricing information included in the SOC.  I used to drive our DEC sales rep crazy asking
for multiple quotes as I would price out different configurations.  What made it even more fun was that there
usually was some deadline to meet in getting configurations and pricing.

Since Compaq no longer provides the SOC, and sales reps are few and far between, the only source of information
for the masses are the WEB pages.  So having a good web design is even more important.

If you want to see how good your web layout is, do this test.  Take an old SOC and pick a system, then
configure it.  Now take and existing system and try to configure it.  See how long it takes.  See if you can
even do it.  The WEB based solution should be faster. If not then it needs to be improved.

The SOC also had very good comparison charts for disks, tapes and system.  Are these consistently accessible
from the web?

Look at the old Digital main page.  It was easy to get to products.  Look at the Compaq main page.  How quickly
can you get to OpenVMS or Alpha products?  How prominent are they?  Alpha systems account for over 50% of
Compaq sales.  Shouldn't they have more web area on the main page?

There is no reason for 404 errors.  There is software available for web servers that track these errors.  Saw
it at DECUS 98.  Not only can you see what URL they wanted to access but also what URL they were requesting it
from.  Using this software would very quickly point out all the broken links on your servers.

If you want faster and more direct input then put a mail link on each page for web page comments

I hope these suggestions are helpful.

Regards,

Cass Witkowski
Senior Systems Engineer
SAIC
witkowskif"at"SAIC.COM










