Lots of questions.  I'll be brief, assuming you understand each point.  If
not ask for help on the not understood point.

1) Get the VMS FAQ, there might (probably is) be some good info on the
   VAXstation 4000 model 60.

2) The system will boot in 2 different manners, in 'server' mode with a
   terminal for a console, and in a workstation mode using monitor, mouse,
   key board, etc.  For ease of checking out the system, the server mode
   is my recomendation.  For this, face the front of the box, pull open
   the small door on the lower right (as you are facing the box) corner.
   Ha, might not have known about that door, huh?  There are several
   switches, jacks, and such.  One switch should be labeled 'S3'.  With
   this switch in the up position, it's a server, in the down position,
   it's a workstation.

3) On the back of the box there is one MMJ (modified modular jack)
   ((I think)).  It's in the bottom center, and has a printer and
   bidirectional lines by it.  There's also a 25 pin DB25 connector
   in the same location.  Connect the MMJ to a VT terminal (or terminal
   emulator on a PC) with the serial line speed set to 9600.  Use
   a null-modem type of cable for the connection.

4) Fire the sucker up, if you get power-on diagnostics and finally a '>>>'
   prompt, or it progresses on to a VMS startup, you're in the door.

5) Other pertinent info on back of box.  Upper left corner, Centronics 50-pin
   SCSI connector.  Bottom left, keyboard and mouse jacks, workstation mode
   only.  On the right, there is both an AUI and a 10base2 (thinwire) jack.
   The slide switch chooses the live jack, make sure the chossen jack is
   connected to an ethernet, ot that a loopback device is on the jack.
   (Easiest is the thinwire, with a 'T' and 2 terminators on the 'T'.

It's a nice machine, give it a chance and you'll be glad you did.

And, get the FAQ, really, get them!

Dave

