nm@vt.edu heeft geschreven in bericht <38544372.244833953@news.vt.edu>...
>It seems that the DEC 3000/300 systems take true parity, fpm, 8MB or
>32MB simms (in matched pairs).  I have a pair of 32MB simms that
>appear as 8MB simms to the DEC.  I read somewhere that this is because
>the 32MB flavor need to have a special resistor attached to them in
>order for them to be detected properly.

Standard PS/2 Simm's have four Presence Detection bits. Two bits are
used to indicate the access time and the other two the memory size.
Normally you have at one end of the Simm (near pin 72) two rows of four
copper solder pads. One row is connected to Vss (GND) and the other
row of individual pads to pins 67 (PRD1) - 68 (PRD2) - 69 (PRD3) -
70 (PRD4). By bridging a pair of pads with a small value resistor (or a
drop of solder) you ground that particular bit. Here is a list of all
possible
combinations :

PRD1    PRD2         mem. size
---------------------------------------------
GND      GND         4 or 64 Mbyte
Open     GND         2 or 32 Mbyte
GND      Open         1 or 16 Mbyte
Open     Open         8 Mbyte


PRD3    PRD4          access time
-------------------------------------------------
GND      GND            50 or 100 nsec
Open     GND            80 nsec
GND      Open           70 nsec
Open     Open           60 nsec

If you have Simm's where those Presence Detect bits are not implemented
but the DEC 3000 is using them to determine the memory size it will indeed
detect them as 8 Mbyte ones  because PRD1 and PRD2 are both open.
With many Simm's these bits are not used but the copper pads are present.
In that case you can just short the PRD2 pads with a drop of solder. If
those pads are not present you can solder a piece of thin insulated wire
from pin 68 to pin 72.

        Good luck ,    Dolf

