In article <FMJo77.Knw@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>
>I have to agree. The site well and truly reeks. I lost track of the number
>of click-thrus I have to do to get info on Alpha or OVMS or whatever, but
>the number is substantial.
>
>Alas, the Q fails to realize that the competition is just a click away!

The really appalling thing is that their customers think and say that the
site stinks and not only does the site not improve, if anything, it gets 
worse by the week as they break more and more of the old Digital links. 
From this state of affairs we can assume that one or more of the following
are true: 

1.  The information has not percolated to the appropriate decision making
    parties (possibly because of the "messengers bearing bad news" effect.)
2.  The appropriate parties have decided that the users are wrong. ("What's
    wrong with the site?  It _looks_ good to me.")
3.  The appropriate parties are studying the problem and plan on 
    implementing a fix "3rd quarter, 2000".
4.  The appropriate parties have ok'd a fix but for some reason it is
    going forward at a pace so glacial that none of us can detect it.

Read it any way you want but to me it spells "bad management".

Come to think of it, just about everything surrounding Compaq these days
says that.  Off the top of my head, and from a personal viewpoint:

1.  The four month wait for a DS10 delivery (plus one more for the final
    PAKs).
2.  The sad handling of the NT/Alpha affair.
3.  The needless destruction of the Digital web site.  It's one thing to 
    migrate it, or to replace it with something equivalent, but to trash
    it with no replacement?
4.  The false Open3D "support" on the Elsa card - where the DS10 web pages
    say (said? I have not checked back lately) it is supported and in reality
    it isn't. 
5.  The "linux drives alpha volume" strategy.  Yet the price of the 
    compilers has not yet been determined and the prices of the machines
    themselves are not competitive against Athlon and PIII systems except
    for floating point bound applications.  That is, the failure to 
    realize that a volume Alpha/linux market will only happen if/when
    chip and board prices drop into the vicinity of PIII and soon, Itanium,
    machines.
6.  Advertising which will not sell product because it is highly unlikely
    that the people who see it (if any) will understand which product it
    is for, or be able to find out starting from the advertising, and in
    any case, won't be able to afford it even if they wanted it.  They may
    not even be able to figure out how to buy it even, if after all that,
    they still do want it.
7.  TCO mania.  Anybody who has ever had to place an order can tell you
    that it is Total Cost of Entry (TCE) which sets market share.
    TCO right now is just one of Compaq's excuses for high sales margins
    which the stock holders like, but the customers hate, as all it means
    is that they pay more for less. The other fallacy in TCO is that you're
    going to keep your systems for a long time when the useful life for
    most computers is at most 3 years.  TCO might save you money but you
    pay a price by spending most of your time running slower, older,
    hardware.  Get TCE in line and THEN TCO may make additional sales. 
8.  Sluggish response to external problems of all types.  When was the last
    time you said to yourself, "Man, those Compaq guys really jumped on
    that problem and took care of it!" (Note, I'm NOT referring to technical
    support problems such as the recent unpriv'd user crashing system bug
    which was handled by the OpenVMS engineers with all due speed.)
9.  The ossified ESL/CSLG program.  Visit

       http://www.digital.com/education/

    While it says "Updated: 28 December 1998" basically nothing has
    changed for more years than I can count.  Well, if you don't count
    all the software which has dropped out of the program.

Not much point going on with this list of infamy.  

At least it's gratifying that the stock market isn't fooled by Compaq - so
the bigwigs who should be fixing all of this, but have not, won't be
cashing in unearned "performance bonuses" anytime soon. 

Regards,

David Mathog
