system@SendSpamHere.ORG (Brian Schenkenberger, VAXman-) writes:
> In article <1999Dec12.160509.1@eisner>, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
> >A great example of why disabling JavaScript is a good idea.
> 
> Java I fear.  JavaScript, however, is relatively benign unless you can 
> provide me with something to dispell my convictions.

Java at least has a security model, and because of this most of the
vulnerabilities that have been discovered have been implementation
bugs, not design errors.  This at least holds out some promise that
java security might improve over time (and also explains why java
security is a more interesting subject for academics than javascript).

JavaScript, on the other hand, not only was born without a security
model, but worse *breaks* the browser's security model in fundamental
and irremediable ways, through the ability to simulate user actions.
There have been many, many javascript security problems found, for
example:

http://www.cen.uiuc.edu/~ejk/browser-security.html
http://www-db.research.bell-labs.com/user/anupam/vulnerability/
http://www.whitehats.com/guninski/netscape.html
http://www.schooner.com/~loverso/javascript/
http://www.digicrime.com/exploits.html
http://www.because-we-can.com/
http://www.shout.net/~nothing/son-of-cache-cow/index.html
http://pages.whowhere.com/computers/cuartangojc/index.html

Note particularly the trouble vendors had closing some of these holes,
with several iterations required to get the problem patched--due at
least in part to the lack of a comprehensive security model.  I have
every expectation that security problems with javascript will continue
to be discovered until it is reengineered from the ground up.

There has been some work on JavaScript security for Mozilla:

http://www.bell-labs.com/user/alain/usenix-html/usenix-html.html

However, this work relies on retro-fitting a security policy onto
a codebase that was not designed with security in mind, and for this
reason I expect it will have its share of vulnerabilities as well.
-- 
