In my opinion, the TZ30 has the dubious distinction of being the only
truly
unreliable DLT tape drive.  The drives made before and after it are far
better.  The TZ30 was the only DLT drive that I ever saw that got stuck
with
a tape in motion with no way to stop it.  The only thing to do was power
it
off with the tape moving.  It crinkled up quite a bit of tape inside the
drive!

I don't know if you can do anything short of powering off the drive to
reset
it.  I'm sure you've tried pressing the button several times.  I have a
vague recollection that you need to press it twice quickly to have it
try to
reset once a tape is loaded.  If the drive is in the error state with
all
the lights flashing, I think it will ignore the button.  Once the drive
is
in the error state the drive will leave the tape eject lever locked to
protect the tape and won't allow you to unload it as long as it's
powered
up.  If the tape isn't moving you might not damage anything by powering
it
off.  If the tape is moving, you might need to take the drive apart to
remove the tape after powering it off.  Sometimes this is caused by a
broken
tape leader in the drive.  It is also possible for the leader in the
tape
cartridge to break.

 
