Following up my own posting again by way of a status report:

Hats off to those who said "VCB01 problems!"  And a special bow-and-
scrape of respect to Enrico Badella for recommending

http://x43.deja.com/.xp?AN=212488318&CONTEXT=944907445.2002583578&hitnum=0

That page tipped me off (we'll say "reminded me," and assume I knew it once
but had forgotten!) about the diagnostic LEDs on the edge of the VCB02 card
itself.  I opened up the VS, fired it up, and looked at the LEDs.  The
pattern was
that for "Second VCB04 4-plane module fault!"

Rather than risk possibly worsening the problem by pulling the VCB04 board
in
question, I noted that I could merely un-ribbon-cable it.  Powered down,
uncabled
that board, powered back up-- and VOILA!  The VS is back in business,
DECwin-
dows and all!  Albeit now with only 16 colors rather than 256... :-(

Next chance I get, I will pull-and-reseat the "problem" board and/or any
socketed
chips thereon, and see if it "comes back to life."  If THAT doesn't work,
though --
dare I ask how much one of these boards costs to replace?  And where, if
any-
where, one might be found?

While we're at it (i.e. while I have the Q-bus cover off!), this reminds
me -- I added
the ESDI disk and corresponding Emulex QD-25 controller from another
MicroVAX
into the VS II/GPX several months ago.  Got that to work, then noticed that
the seri-
al devices had disappeared.  Evidently the CSR-and/or-vector of the QD25
con-
flicts with that of the serial card.  No big deal: disengaging the QD25 long
enough
to let VMS "see" the serial card again, SYSGEN reports it as a DHV11; I have
the
VS II/GPX configuration manual which includes a page or two on setting the
DHV11
CSR and vector.

Unfortunately, the card I have is demonstrably NOT a REAL DHV11: mine is
half-
height whereas the DHV11 is said to be QUAD-height.  Fortunately, mine says
on
it, "DHQ11" on it, and I was able to locate some old Usenet postings about
config-
uring a DHQ11.  Unfortunately, even THOSE descriptions don't correspond to
MY
card -- perhaps because, in full, what it says on it is "CAMINTONN
CM-DHQ11" --
is it an emulation of an emulation?  A "fourth-party" card as it were?  Hoo
boy.  In
any case, anybody know how to configure this puppy?  It's got two 11-slot
jumper
banks which I take as representing CSR-and-vector-like settings, but I have
not
been able to produce any change in the SYSGEN display over several
iterations
of adding a single additional jumper to any of several different
currently-unjumpered
positions.  I could do it by trial-and-error, certainly, but that'll take
time and exert a
lot of wear-and-tear on the card edge, backplane slot, serial offtake ribbon
cables,
etc...   It would be infinitely preferable to have some advance information
"going in."
Anybody?

Thanks again for all help thus far, and in advance for all yet to appear.

Chris





