Bob Hassinger wrote:
> I was under the impression the .COM issue was on the client side, not the server. IIRC IE uses the file name extension
> to decide what to do with a  file and does respect the MIME type (or some such).

Not, IE doesn't respect the mime type. If the extension is SWF and the mime
type is "text/plain", then IE still calls the plug in for the flash player. IE
seems to disregard the mime type if an extension is supplied.

So no matter how you configure the web server, the IE will disregard the mime
type supplied by the server.

Personally, I think it is absolutely ludicrous that Microsoft would configure
a client to execute binary code right off the web (.COM files).

The Macinstosh Netscape, on the other hand, relies exclusively on the mime
type. a .SWF file with a text/plain mime type will result in the binary data
being displayed. Many sites that use FLASH are not aware that their flash
animations don't work on half the workstations because they only tested the MS
stuff and are clueless that the mime type is not set properly.



