This used to be a fairly common behavior of some PC VGA monitors.

My AOC CM-324, for instance. Behaves _exactly_ as you describe yours, except
mine has "H. CENTER" and "V. SIZE" easily accessible on the front :)

Basically, when you change the horiz/vert scan rates (which change with
resolution), certain characteristics of the monitor's circuitry change. Many
multiscan monitors have (or had when I got into this stuff) ways of
compensating for this, often using a relay to switch supply voltages, etc.,
but some cheaper ones didn't, and the higher resolutions they claimed to
support (my AOC's 800x600 comes to mind) required the user to tweak some
knobs.

Not knowing anything about  your monitor specifically, my guesses would be:

A) Your monitor has some sort of automatic switch and multiple adjustments,
one set for each resolution (this is pretty common in older SVGAs), and the
set for the res you're using has drifted. The solution would be to remove
the back and locate the right ones, then adjust them.

B) Your monitor doesn't have the automatic switching, and the company didn't
think out their design too well, not making the controls accessible to the
user. (or simply listing the monitor as not supporting that res so they
wouldn't have to deal with it). In this case, there's probably one set of
internal adjustments, which you can set to work properly with one res, but
your adjustments will affect lower res's as well, so some will come out too
vertically small and shifted off to the right :)

>a) the picuture is shifted to the left quite a bit and
>b) it is too large vertically, i.e. one does not see the lowest nor
>topmost rows, which is quite annoying.




