Georg Schwarz (schwarz@physik.tu-berlin.de) wrote:
: We have connected an old digital (DEC) 290-D3 19" color monitor to 
: DECstation 5000/125. The monitor is said to have come from a VAX.
: To the best of my knowledge it runs at 1024x768, 60 Hz, Sync on Green.
: It has three BNC connectors (RGB).
: We get a stable picture and nice colors, but
: a) the picuture is shifted to the left quite a bit and
: b) it is too large vertically, i.e. one does not see the lowest nor
: topmost rows, which is quite annoying.
: Is this because the monitor is misaligned, or does it expect somewhat
: different timing/border sizes/...?
: Is there a way to adjust/resize the picture? Some controls inside? There
: don't seem to be controls for size or position on the outside.

You don't indicate the video card in the DECstation.  There are several video
cards available.  It may be that the one you have is not compatible with the
VR290-D3 monitor.

Generally with the VR290 monitors, if you have a compatible video card and
the image is not correctly displayed, you'll need to open the monitor and make
some adjustments.  The only externally accessible adjustments are brightness
and contrast.  Clearly, your monitor needs adjustment on horizontal position
and vertical height.  When you adjust the vertical height, you'll likely need
to adjust the vertical linearity, too.

So, start by looking at the video card.  On the back of the system, next to
the video connector, there is generally the model number of the video card,
typically starting with "PMAG...".  For your monitor, the PMAG-B is one
compatible card.  Also, from the console prompt, you can type:

        >> cnfg
        
and find the video card in the listing.  Again, the video card model numbers
start with PMAG...

        Stu



