In article <gmc44.13915$wG3.920425@typhoon.nyroc.rr.com>, "Chris Chiesa" <cchiesa1@rochester.rr.com> writes:
:   Well, not QUITE.  Although I get no text on the graphics-head monitor, I
:do seem to get "raster" -- i.e. the monitor is working.  I also don't seem 
:to get any text on a serial terminal connected to the rear-panel 
:serial-console port -- though I'm not 100% positive I have it connected 
:100% correctly...

  The DB9 serial line pinout is in the OpenVMS FAQ...

:  The only "useful" information I have is that
:the rear-panel LED display shows the letter "A."
:
:   A KA-630 manual that probably didn't come with the VS itself says that
:the letter "A" indicates problems with the VCB01 graphics subsystem; only, 
:I'm not sure whether I have a KA-630 CPU per se, and I definitely have a 
:VCB02, not VCB01, graphics subsystem.

  The KA630 "A" self-test code indicates a problem with the graphics 
  controller or associated graphics hardware, be that the VCB01 (QVSS) 
  or the VCB02 (QDSS)...

:   So... can anyone shed any light on what's going on here, and how to fix
:it?  Thanks in advance!

  Well, as a start, re-seat the Q-bus boards and check the cables...

  You may end up swapping the VCB02.  (There are various revisions of the 
  VCB02 around, and some older revisions can lock up due to Q-bus timing 
  issues -- I saw one VCB02 suddenly develop this problem after months of 
  correct operation, and I ended up wiring in an FCO to "adjust" the bus 
  timing...  All better...  In my experience, this VCB02 lock-up was rare
  with the KA630, and was somewhat more common with the faster KA650 and 
  KA655 processors...)

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




