Where I come from, people look up words in the dictionary if they want
to know what they mean, rather than speculating endlessly based on
their fuzzy memories or whatever.

bald-faced \'bo[0xC7]l(d)-'fa^-st\ adj
(1943)
:BAREFACED 

bare-faced \'ba(e)r-'fa^-st, 'be(e)r-\ adj
(1590)
1: having the face uncovered:
1a: having no beard or whiskers: BEARDLESS
1b: wearing no mask
2a: OPEN, UNCONCEALED
2b: lacking scruples
-- bare-faced-ly \-'fa^--sed-le^-, -'fa^-st-le^-\ adv 
-- bare-faced-ness \-'fa^--sed-nes, -'fa^-s(t)-nes\ n 

bold-faced \'bo^-l(d)-'fa^-st\ adj
(1591)
1: bold in manner or conduct: IMPUDENT
2 usu bold.faced: being or set in boldface 

im-pu-dent \-ent\ adj
[ME, fr. L impudent-, impudens, fr. in- + pudent-, pudens, prp.
     of pude^-re to feel shame]
(14c)
1 obs: lacking modesty
2: marked by contemptuous or cocky boldness or disregard of others: INSOLENT
-- im-pu-dent-ly adv 


From the online Webster's entries, I would say that definitions 2a and
2b of bare-faced are much closer to the intended meaning than
definition 1 of bold-faced. I have heard of someone being accused of
telling an open, unconcealed lie, not even having the scruples to hide
it, but I don't know if I have heard of someone telling an impudent lie.
I suppose someone could be "cocky" or "insolent" when lying, but that
doesn't sound like the meaning that was intended above.

Anyway, I did learn from the dictionary that "bare-faced" preceded
"bald-faced" by several centuries.

Back to your regularly scheduled flamewar.





