Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Jay,

the cheapest way to connect your systems over FDDI is to use concentrators.
Since you run them in a ring, I assume that all nodes have a DAS FDDI
interface.
This means that you're best solution comprises two concentrators: one in
which you
plug in the B-port of a DAS, the other for the A-port. The two concentrators
must be  in
a (small) ring.

Now DEC/Cabletron/Compaq has two concentrators listed: a six port 900MX (A,B
4*M)
and a 900MH (?) which has 12 SC connectors.
Note: the 900MX comes without PMD's, which gives you a choice of MIC, SC or
UTP PMD's
(at around $500 a piece). The 900MH has SC connectors fitted (no choice).

They either use a DEbub one for power (one H1 for each) or a MS900 chassis
which comes
in an 8 slot and a 4-slot version. The MS900 chassis allows you to use all
six MIC outlets as
M-ports and put the connecting A&B ports on the backplane. So there is a pay
back for the
900 chassis over the much cheaper hub-one.

OBR's require a special outlet on the FDDI device to switch the OBR. I've
seen
OBR ports on 900 series modules but not on a DEC built FDDI interface, nor
on the
GigaSwitch. BTW, Compaq/DEC does  not sell an OBR.

The GigaSwitch is very expensive: a four port linecard is twice as expensive
as a 900MX.
The frame itself is over $18.000 (I'm in the Netherlands, US prices may
differ).
The unit is robust, redundant but *very* expensive. The company I work for
uses 4 of these
beasts and their built quality is excellent.
Used units may be a lot cheaper, I have no idea.

Hans Vlems


