> Gary,
> 
> Consider that the systems that you are comparing
> are vastly different designs. The SP2 is a multi-
> node message passing architecture while the GS320
> is a single shared memory multiprocessor machine.
> What does that mean to you? Consider that if you
> have approximately a 200GB database, the GS320 is
> capable of caching the ENTIRE database in its
> main memory. All references to the database would
> occur through low latency, high bandwidth shared
> memory references within a single box. About five
> years ago, when Digital introduced the first
> system in the GS140 family the Very Large Memory
> (VLM) attribute of that system demonstrated huge
> performance speedups (on the order of 10-100
> times) on commercial applications versus IBM
> mainframe systems (SP1 systems I believe).
> 
> On an SP2 system, your database will be
> distributed across a number of interconnected
> boxes (the largest of which can support about an
> eighth of your database). Data sharing and
> intercommunication will take place through higher
> latency, limited bandwidth messages passed
> between the boxes.
> 
> It is also worth noting that if it is a message
> passing architecture that you are looking for,
> Compaq has recently announced a the AlphaServer
> SC series of systems. These are message passing
> systems of the same flavor as the SP2. These
> systems use the Quadrix Super Computing World's
> (QSW) super high performance switch technology to
> interconnect up to (I believe) 128 AlphaServer
> nodes. The system also includes some slick
> software for things like "single system" system
> management. And it can be used to interconnect
> any AlphaServer systems (though technically not
> unannounced ones :-) ).
> 
> BTW if you do look into the GS140 numbers, they
> may be impressive, but they will understate the
> capabilities of the GS320 family.
> 
